content.text:
All three removals are exactly as scoped, `npx tsc --noEmit` is clean, and the full suite passes. One thing surfaced that needs your call before this step is "done" in the strictest sense — see below.

## What I changed

File: `/Users/marcel.a.n.navarro/Dev/zimmermann-pwa/overrides/app/pages/product-detail/index.tsx` — the only file touched, nothing staged/committed.

**Removal 1 — the interim hook's import (was line 63):**
```diff
 import { getConfig } from '@salesforce/pwa-kit-runtime/utils/ssr-config';
-import { useRecentlyViewedHistory } from '../../hooks/useRecentlyViewedHistory';
 import { APP_MAIN_BLEED_MX } from '../../constants';
```

**Removal 2 — the hook call (was line 807):**
```diff
   // effect immediately below.
-  const { markProductViewed } = useRecentlyViewedHistory();

   /**************** Einstein ****************/
```

**Removal 3 — the write inside the Einstein effect (was line 839):**
```diff
       activeData.sendViewProduct(category, product, 'detail');
       dataCloud.sendViewProduct(product);
     }
-    if (product) markProductViewed(product.id);
   }, [product]);
```

`git diff --stat`: `1 file changed, 3 deletions(-)` — no insertions, no other line moved, matching the plan's pass criterion literally.

## Evidence per pass criterion (plan.md E3, lines 259-263)

- **"`git diff` shows only those three removals; no other line in the file moved"** — confirmed: `git diff --stat` shows `3 deletions(-)`, 0 insertions, and the diff hunks above are the only three.
- **"`einstein.sendViewProduct` / `dataCloud.sendViewProduct` calls are byte-identical to before"** — confirmed by grepping both terms post-edit: all 10 occurrences remain (product-set branch, child-product loop, single-product branch), none reworded or reordered.
- **"`npx tsc --noEmit` + `npm run test` green"** — `npx tsc --noEmit` produced no output (clean, 0 errors). `npm run test`: **87 test files, 1324 tests, all passed**, including `recipeGuards.test.ts` (6/6 — untouched since this step made no styling change) and `product-detail`'s two existing test files (`productInfo.test.ts`, `variantScopedPrice.test.ts`, both green, unaffected). The stderr `MISSING_TRANSLATION` lines in the output are pre-existing fallback-message warnings from an unrelated `recentlyViewed` test, not failures — the run summary confirms 87/87 and 1324/1324 passed.
- **"`grep -n "markProductViewed\|useRecentlyViewedHistory" product-detail/index.tsx` → zero hits"** — does **not** pass cleanly: it returns **one** hit, on line 803, inside the pre-existing explanatory comment ("`... so `markProductViewed` (this browser's own interim view-history stand-in) is obtained here...`"). See below — I did not resolve this myself.

## The one thing I did not decide for you

Consequence: the plan's own two E3 requirements conflict with each other, and satisfying one breaks the other — nobody will notice until a scorer runs the grep literally.

Mechanism: E3's write-up says two things at once: "exactly three lines, no more" (with `git diff` showing only those three removals as a pass criterion), *and* a grep that must return zero hits for `markProductViewed`. The four-line comment directly above the deleted `const { markProductViewed } = ...` line explains *why* that line existed — and it still contains the literal word `markProductViewed` in prose. Deleting it would satisfy the grep but touch a fourth line (contradicting the "exactly three, no more" instruction repeated three times in the plan and in the task I was given). Leaving it satisfies "exactly three lines" but fails the grep as literally written.

Fix (not applied — your call): either (a) leave it as I have — three lines only, and treat the grep pass criterion as satisfied "in spirit" (no functional/executable reference remains, only prose) — or (b) also remove those four comment lines now as a fourth, tightly-scoped edit, or (c) defer the comment cleanup to E4, which already has a "sweep stale comment references" line-item but currently does not name `product-detail/index.tsx` in its list of locations to sweep. I did not pick one — this is exactly the kind of plan-internal contradiction I was told to surface rather than resolve silently.

## Scope discipline

Only `product-detail/index.tsx` was touched. I did not delete `useRecentlyViewedHistory.ts` or its test (confirmed still present, still referenced by `constants/index.ts`, `recentlyViewedProductFetch.ts`, and `zimmRecentlyViewedRail/index.tsx` — all already named as E4's job in the plan, not new information). I did not touch `recentlyViewedProductFetch.ts`, `recentlyViewedCard.ts`, `recentlyViewedFullHistoryCard.ts`, `constants/index.ts`, or anything under `recommended-products/`/`recentlyViewed/`. I did not run `cr review`/CodeRabbit or `npm run lint`/`audit:tokens`/`npm run build` — the plan places those at the "Final gate" before opening the PR (once, after E4), not per-step, and E3 is explicitly "implementer + scorer only, mechanical" with no tester round-trip — running the full gate now would be over-scaled effort for a 3-line deletion.

## Unverified gaps

- I have not verified `einstein.sendViewProduct`'s runtime behaviour (only that its source text is unchanged) — Einstein is undeployed everywhere reachable per the plan, so no live/rendered check was possible for this or any step in this sequence.
- I did not check whether any other file outside this branch's diff (e.g. a Storybook story or a Playwright spec) references `product-detail/index.tsx`'s removed symbols — the task named this as out of scope, so I did not grep the wider `e2e/` tree.

## What Marcel is missing

The grep-vs-line-count contradiction above is the main one. Separately: E3's own "why it's needed" note says removing this write-path "keeps `useRecentlyViewedHistory` alive and un-deletable" if left — worth double-checking before E4 runs that `zimmRecentlyViewedRail/index.tsx`'s reference to `RECENTLY_VIEWED_HISTORY_MAX_STORED` (comment-only per Open Question 4) doesn't hide a second, non-comment usage that would block that hook's deletion the same way this file's did.
